From: Abue Ammar <14371506+abue-ammar@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 06:44:13 +0600
Subject: [PATCH] Animate the first menu after the palette closes with one open
 (#1644)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A child window that AppKit orders out together with its parent skips
its next open animation. Choosing a ⌘K action closes the palette with
the menu still attached, so the first menu of the next session appeared
without the bounce. PalettePanel now detaches and hides its children
before ordering itself out.
---
 Tinycast/Palette/PalettePanel.swift | 9 +++++++++
 docs/features/palette.md            | 3 ++-
 2 files changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Tinycast/Palette/PalettePanel.swift b/Tinycast/Palette/PalettePanel.swift
index 9a91d383..de939914 100644
--- a/Tinycast/Palette/PalettePanel.swift
+++ b/Tinycast/Palette/PalettePanel.swift
@@ -287,6 +287,15 @@ final class PalettePanel: NSPanel {
         paletteState?.noteCommandHeld(false)
     }
 
+    /// A child hidden with its parent skips its next open animation.
+    override func orderOut(_ sender: Any?) {
+        for child in childWindows ?? [] {
+            removeChildWindow(child)
+            child.orderOut(nil)
+        }
+        super.orderOut(sender)
+    }
+
     override var canBecomeKey: Bool { true }
     // The parent stays main while a menu is key, so its Liquid Glass remains active.
     override var canBecomeMain: Bool { true }
diff --git a/docs/features/palette.md b/docs/features/palette.md
index b1175c53..e6a837ea 100644
--- a/docs/features/palette.md
+++ b/docs/features/palette.md
@@ -478,7 +478,8 @@ width; their hover keeps the shared 10pt menu-row corner.
 
 A menu is **not** an overlay inside the palette: `MenuPanelController` hosts it in a `MenuPanel`, a
 borderless non-activating `NSPanel` added as a **child window** of the palette's, which is what makes
-it follow a palette drag and vanish with it. Glass renders against the desktop rather than inside an
+it follow a palette drag and vanish with it. `PalettePanel.orderOut` detaches every child first,
+because a child ordered out along with its parent skips its next open animation. Glass renders against the desktop rather than inside an
 already-blurred, clipped panel, and no menu can be cropped by `RootPaletteView`'s `clipShape` however
 long it grows. The menu temporarily becomes key so its native `TextField` owns the caret and selection, while
 `MenuPanel` hands navigation and action shortcuts back to `RootPaletteView`. It restores key status
